# SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
#
# PROBLEM: the word "enter" carries three roles — a verb, a key
# name, and a key token. One concept (the Enter key), three
# spellings, and the same action writable three ways.

description "The three spellings of the Enter key"
platform    dos
timeout     30s

# Role 1 — the verb: type guest text, then press the Enter key.
enter "dir"

# Role 2 — the key name: a bare word in press's key vocabulary.
press enter

# Role 3 — the key token: quoted markup inside a typed string.
type "<enter>"

# By definition, these two lines are the same action:
enter "cls"
type  "cls<enter>"

# The collision at a glance: three consecutive lines, and the
# reader must know that the first IMPLIES the key, the second NAMES
# it, and the third QUOTES it —
enter "exit"        # guest text + the key
press enter         # just the key
type  "<enter>"     # also just the key

# The asymmetry runs deeper: `enter` is reserved as an identifier
# (no phase may be named enter), yet it is an ordinary word in the
# key namespace one token after `press`. Reserved in one namespace,
# plain in another (see 08-bare-word-namespaces.rlqs).
#
# OPEN QUESTION: rename the verb so "enter" appears in exactly one
# lexical class? Candidates: `submit "dir"` (verb-first, honest
# about the implied keypress), `line "dir"`, or dropping the verb
# entirely — `type "dir<enter>"` is only three characters longer
# and single-role. The verb earns its keep only if install scripts
# use it constantly (they do — see design-install.rlqs `shutdown`).
